comment -- The ludo reference program.
comment --
comment -- Grown one chapter at a time (ADR-0044 §7). Chapter 1 seeds it with the
comment -- lexical and grammatical surface and nothing else: every construct below is
comment -- spelled by docs/spec/01-grammar.md and parses against docs/spec/grammar.ebnf.
comment -- Chapter 2 adds the constructs whose *meaning* it fixes — absence, failure,
comment -- the bug line, generics, function types, and text. Chapter 3 adds memory —
comment -- layout, the two aggregates and their literal, views and the rules that keep
comment -- them from dangling, moves, and multiple returns.
comment --
comment -- The dynamic container set is now fixed at `List` + `Map` (ch3 §11.3), so this
comment -- program exercises it: a fallible `push`, a `reserve` before a batch, and a
comment -- `persist` container. What it still does not name is a pool.
comment --
comment -- Container *removal* is specified by ch3 §11.13-§11.13.9, with the descending
comment -- range operator `>..` that its cull needs (ch1 §7.7.1), and this program now
comment -- exercises it in four shapes: the pool's hand-written swap-down (`despawn`),
comment -- the descending `swap_remove` cull (`cull`), the order-preserving pack-forward
comment -- (`compact`), and `truncate(0)` per-frame reuse of a `persist` buffer
comment -- (`marks`). The extension landed as #109 under ADR-0049 §4, which lets a large
comment -- reference-program extension become its own work while the clause repair never
comment -- splits.
comment --
comment -- `trail` is **bounded** and no longer the leak it was, at the cost the spec
comment -- states rather than hides: the cap keeps the oldest entries, which is the
comment -- wrong end for a trail and the stated absence at ch3 §11.13.9 (issue #108).
comment --
comment -- Until a frontend exists this file is checked by hand. It is written fresh:
comment -- prototypes/07-syntax/b-lua-flavoured.ludo is not a starting point, and breaks
comment -- six ways against the current corpus (ADR-0044 §7).
comment -- The reserved root is enumerated by ch6 §12, and this program now writes the
comment -- three surfaces that enumeration authored: `$.assert` in `despawn`, `$.panic`
comment -- in `spawn`, and `$.ivec2` in `cell_of`. Until #119 none of the three had a
comment -- spelling, so the language's own bug-declaring call was unwritable here.
comment --
comment -- Chapter 6 replaces the hand-rolled `Vec2` with the blessed `$.vec2`, and adds
comment -- the facades: the program now draws, plays a voice, reads input, reads a video
comment -- preference and writes a storage slot.
comment --
comment -- Chapter 5 adds the runner: what runs when, and what survives. Top level was
comment -- already here and unexplained; now it is the entry file's top level, running
comment -- once before any frame (ch5 §2.2, §2.4). The canvas is declared at that top
comment -- level and is immutable for the process's life (ch5 §9.3, §9.4); a sprite is
comment -- a declaration the runner resolves before top level runs, not a call (ch5
comment -- §8.1, §8.3); the per-frame arena is finally used (ch5 §3.10); and the one
comment -- decision this chapter had to make -- every `persist` declaration carries an
comment -- initialiser (ch5 §4.3.1) -- is shown at `name` below, which is the line
comment -- ADR-0045 §8 wrote without one.
comment -- Chapter 4 makes this file the **entry file of a program with three modules**,
comment -- which is the whole of what it adds. `libs/vec-utils/` is a library because it
comment -- claims a name; `hud.ludo` is this program's own file and is reached by path;
comment -- the boundary between the two forms is the chapter's subject (ch4 §3.1).
comment --
comment -- A library is named, never located: nothing below says where `vec-utils` is,
comment -- and moving it -- to a cache, to a vendor directory -- changes no line here
comment -- (ch4 §3.3). The runner finds it by scanning `libs/` because no build tool
comment -- supplied a mapping (ch4 §8.1).
comment -- Chapter 6 makes this a program that **draws, plays, reads input and saves**.
comment -- Everything it adds is a name under `$.`: the blessed math set replaces the
comment -- hand-rolled `Vec2`, `$.graphics` draws against the `Target` the runner hands
comment -- the frame entry, `$.input` is read as an ambient per-frame constant,
comment -- `$.audio` plays one voice, `$.video` reports a preference the program does
comment -- not own, and `$.storage` writes a slot declared like an asset.
use
string "vec-utils"
comment -- A path reference names one file, extension written, relative to this file's
comment -- directory, and binds the file's stem (ch4 §3.4). This spelling is authored by
comment -- chapter 4 §15.1: ADR-0014 §7 fixed the rule and never wrote the form.
use
string "./hud.ludo"
const
ident MAX_ROCKS
=
int 64
comment -- A storage slot is declared, never opened: `extern` is reused and `storage`
comment -- occupies the kind-noun slot, so the core grammar gains nothing and there is
comment -- no path, no directory and no runtime key anywhere in this program
comment -- (ch6 §8.1, §8.2).
extern
ident storage
ident save1
comment -- An asset is *declared*, never loaded: the name is fixed at compile time, so a
comment -- typo is a compile error rather than a path that fails at 3am, and there is no
comment -- load call to put a string inside (ch5 §8.1). The path is relative to this
comment -- file and may not cross a library boundary (ch5 §8.2).
comment --
comment -- The runner resolves and decodes it *before* top level runs, so this program
comment -- can never observe a loading state -- there is no `?Image`, no readiness poll
comment -- and no progress value (ch5 §8.3). A missing file is a fault at top level and
comment -- the simulation never starts (ch5 §8.4); editing the file while the program
comment -- runs swaps the bytes at the next frame boundary, with `persist` untouched
comment -- (ch5 §8.6).
extern
ident image
ident rock
=
string "art/rock.png"
