comment -- The sanctioned cull, and the first `>..` in this program (ch1 §7.7.1).
comment --
comment -- **The direction is the correctness condition, not a style.** `swap_remove(i)`
comment -- moves the last element into slot `i`; an *ascending* cursor has already
comment -- passed that slot and never looks at the moved element again, so a rock that
comment -- should have died survives -- silently, with no fault and no diagnostic. That
comment -- is the failure class ch1 §13.9.1 charges the operator's budget overrule
comment -- against: `>..` exists because `for i in 0..<rocks.len` is the shape a program
comment -- reaches for and the shape that is wrong.
comment --
comment -- `rocks.len>..0` yields `len-1` down to `0`, and the length is the excluded
comment -- bound in both directions, which is what makes it the always-correct
comment -- descending shape (ch1 §7.7.1). The loop's subject is a `RevRange`, so **no
comment -- lend on `rocks` exists** and §12.2's ban on structural mutation during
comment -- iteration has nothing to fire on (ch3 §12.2.1) -- which is the entire reason
comment -- the cull iterates a range rather than the container.
comment --
comment -- The returned element is discarded with the one greppable token: removal
comment -- returns what it removed (ch3 §11.13.2), and must-use is discharged, not
comment -- ignored.
