comment -- **The bound, and the honest note about which end it keeps.** Before this
comment -- line the program pushed two points every 60 ticks forever: a live leak in
comment -- the spec's own reference program, growing without limit for as long as it
comment -- runs.
comment --
comment -- `truncate(64)` bounds it, and keeps the **oldest** 64 points -- the wrong
comment -- end for a trail, which wants the most recent. This is not an oversight to
comment -- be worked around here: the mandated container set has **no front-drop and
comment -- no FIFO**, and the ring buffer that would answer it is blessed but
comment -- unmandated, so a portable program cannot rely on it (ch3 §11.13.9,
comment -- issue #108). A hand-rolled shift-down would cost O(n) every frame to
comment -- simulate a container the set deliberately does not mandate.
comment --
comment -- The program therefore takes the bound and states the cost, which is what
comment -- the spec's own exemplar owes a reader who is about to hit this.
ident trail
!
.
ident truncate
(
int 64
)
comment -- Mutable iteration. The mark is *postfix on the loop subject* -- `trail!`,
comment -- never `!trail` -- because the subject is an ordinary expression and the
comment -- mark rides the root binding there as it does everywhere else (ch1 §7.10,
comment -- ch3 §12.2). Prefix `!` is type position only, which is why the declaration
comment -- above reads `persist trail: !List[$.vec2]` and this line does not.
comment --
comment -- The mark fires *once*, at loop entry: the subject is evaluated once, so the
comment -- kill is a single event and the loop does not invalidate `p` on the next
comment -- turn. `p` is a writable view of the element, so `p!.y` is the same shape as
comment -- `advance`'s `r!.pos` one function scope up.
comment --
comment -- Nothing here pushes, removes or truncates: the lend is exclusive for the
comment -- loop's duration, and structural mutation inside it is a compile error
comment -- rather than a documented invalidation hazard (ch3 §12.2).
